WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.817
This lesson becomes much easier when the learner stops seeing energy as one vague number and starts seeing specific stores.

2
00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:11.634
A moving object can store energy as kinetic energy.

3
00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:17.451
A raised object can store energy as gravitational potential energy.

4
00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:23.268
The powerful idea is that energy can shift from one store to another while the quantity stays trackable.

5
00:00:23.268 --> 00:00:29.085
That is why the lesson can compare a trolley moving along the ground with the same trolley climbing a ramp.

6
00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:34.902
Keep this takeaway in view: Think in named energy stores, not just in one anonymous number.

7
00:00:34.902 --> 00:00:40.719
Kinetic energy means motion store.

8
00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:46.536
This store belongs to an object because it is moving.

9
00:00:46.536 --> 00:00:52.353
Gravitational potential means height store.

10
00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:58.170
This store belongs to an object because it is raised in a gravitational field.

11
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.731
This diagram helps because it separates what changes each store.

12
00:01:02.731 --> 00:01:07.292
Mass matters in both.

13
00:01:07.292 --> 00:01:11.853
Speed matters strongly in kinetic energy.

14
00:01:11.853 --> 00:01:16.414
Height matters directly in gravitational potential energy.

15
00:01:16.414 --> 00:01:20.975
The learner should begin to expect those patterns before calculating.

16
00:01:20.975 --> 00:01:25.536
If speed changes, kinetic energy can change sharply.

17
00:01:25.536 --> 00:01:30.097
If height changes, gravitational potential energy changes in a more direct proportional way.

18
00:01:30.097 --> 00:01:34.658
Keep this takeaway in view: Know which variable belongs to which store before you calculate.

19
00:01:34.658 --> 00:01:39.219
Shared variable: Mass appears in both energy expressions.

20
00:01:39.219 --> 00:01:43.780
Motion variable: Speed controls how much kinetic energy the object has.

21
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.932
A useful analogy is to imagine two pockets in the same jacket.

22
00:01:48.932 --> 00:01:54.084
One pocket is labelled motion and the other is labelled height.

23
00:01:54.084 --> 00:01:59.235
The value can move from one pocket to the other, but it is still the same total value being tracked.

24
00:01:59.235 --> 00:02:04.387
That is what happens when a moving trolley climbs a ramp.

25
00:02:04.387 --> 00:02:09.539
Some of the motion-store energy can appear as height-store energy.

26
00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.691
The analogy helps learners keep transfer and storage visible instead of treating every question as unrelated substitution.

27
00:02:14.691 --> 00:02:19.843
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson is about comparing where the energy is stored, not losing track of it.

28
00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:24.995
Motion pocket means kinetic.

29
00:02:24.995 --> 00:02:30.146
Energy here depends strongly on how fast the object is moving.

30
00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:35.298
Height pocket means gravitational.

31
00:02:35.298 --> 00:02:40.450
Energy here depends on how high the object has been raised.

32
00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.055
Kinetic energy is one half m v squared.

33
00:02:45.055 --> 00:02:49.659
Gravitational potential energy is m g h.

34
00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:54.264
These are not random formulas.

35
00:02:54.264 --> 00:02:58.868
They are compact descriptions of two different stores.

36
00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:03.473
The squared speed term is especially important, because it tells you speed has a strong effect on kinetic energy.

37
00:03:03.473 --> 00:03:08.078
The height relation is more direct.

38
00:03:08.078 --> 00:03:12.682
Double the height and, with the same mass and g, the gravitational potential energy doubles.

39
00:03:12.682 --> 00:03:17.287
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the store meaning to decide which relation belongs to the question.

40
00:03:17.287 --> 00:03:21.892
Mass: Mass increases both energy stores.

41
00:03:21.892 --> 00:03:26.496
Speed: Speed changes kinetic energy strongly because of the square.

42
00:03:26.496 --> 00:03:31.101
E_k = 0.5 m v^2.

43
00:03:31.101 --> 00:03:35.705
E_p = m g h.

44
00:03:35.705 --> 00:03:40.310
Near Earth, use g about 10 newtons per kilogram unless told otherwise.

45
00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:45.320
If mass doubles, both stores double.

46
00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:50.330
If height doubles, gravitational potential energy doubles.

47
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:55.340
But if speed doubles, kinetic energy becomes four times larger because the speed is squared.

48
00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.350
That is a very important pattern.

49
00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:05.360
It means speed is often the dominant comparison variable in kinetic-energy questions.

50
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.370
A small change in speed can matter much more than the same small percentage change in mass.

51
00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:15.380
Keep this takeaway in view: Speed is the variable that can make kinetic energy rise very quickly.

52
00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.390
Double mass means double both stores.

53
00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:25.400
Mass scales both energy expressions in a straightforward way.

54
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.410
Double height means double E_p.

55
00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:35.420
Gravitational potential energy changes directly with height.

56
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.188
Try three checks.

57
00:04:39.188 --> 00:04:42.957
Which variable matters in both energy stores?

58
00:04:42.957 --> 00:04:46.725
Mass.

59
00:04:46.725 --> 00:04:50.494
Which variable has the stronger effect in kinetic energy?

60
00:04:50.494 --> 00:04:54.262
Speed, because it is squared.

61
00:04:54.262 --> 00:04:58.031
And if the same object rises higher without changing speed, which store increases?

62
00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:01.799
Gravitational potential energy.

63
00:05:01.799 --> 00:05:05.568
Those checks are enough to make the example feel like one connected comparison rather than two separate equations.

64
00:05:05.568 --> 00:05:09.336
Keep this takeaway in view: You are ready once the variable roles are clearer than the symbols themselves.

65
00:05:09.336 --> 00:05:13.105
Shared factor means mass.

66
00:05:13.105 --> 00:05:16.873
Mass appears in both energy-store relations.

67
00:05:16.873 --> 00:05:20.642
Strong kinetic factor means speed.

68
00:05:20.642 --> 00:05:24.410
The squared speed term makes the effect large.

69
00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:29.483
You are ready for the worked example now.

70
00:05:29.483 --> 00:05:34.556
A 2 kilogram trolley moves at 5 metres per second.

71
00:05:34.556 --> 00:05:39.629
Start with kinetic energy.

72
00:05:39.629 --> 00:05:44.703
One half times 2 times 5 squared gives 25 joules.

73
00:05:44.703 --> 00:05:49.776
Then the question asks for a height with the same amount of gravitational potential energy.

74
00:05:49.776 --> 00:05:54.849
So set m g h equal to 25.

75
00:05:54.849 --> 00:05:59.922
With mass 2 and g 10, that becomes 2 times 10 times h equals 25.

76
00:05:59.922 --> 00:06:04.995
Solving gives h equals 1.25 metres.

77
00:06:04.995 --> 00:06:10.068
The key idea is not only the arithmetic.

78
00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:15.141
It is that the same tracked 25 joules can appear as motion energy or height energy.

79
00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:20.214
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: compute one store, then match it to the other.

80
00:06:20.214 --> 00:06:25.288
Step 1: Find the kinetic energy from the mass and speed.

81
00:06:25.288 --> 00:06:30.361
Step 2: Set the gravitational store equal to that same energy amount.

82
00:06:30.361 --> 00:06:35.434
E_k = 0.5 x 2 x 5^2 = 25 J.

83
00:06:35.434 --> 00:06:40.507
E_p = m g h = 25 J.

84
00:06:40.507 --> 00:06:45.580
Carry the same 25 joules from one store to the other.
